// The Whole Counsel: Genesis pilot, chapters 26-30.
// Question bank only. Verse text is filled later by tools/fill-verses.js (verse:"" stays empty).

VG.registerBibleChapters("genesis", [
{n:26, name:"Isaac in Gerar", seal:{ref:"Genesis 26:24",verse:"And the LORD appeared unto him the same night, and said, I am the God of Abraham thy father: fear not, for I am with thee, and will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham’s sake."}, facts:["Did you know Isaac is the only patriarch who never left the land of Canaan? God told him not to go down into Egypt, and he stayed his whole life.","Did you know the names of Isaac's wells tell a story? Esek means contention, Sitnah means hatred, and Rehoboth means room: the moment strife ended, God made room.","Did you know Beersheba means well of the oath? Isaac's servants found water there the same day he made a covenant with Abimelech."], questions:[
{q:"Why did Isaac journey to Gerar?",options:["A famine in the land","A war with the Philistines","A command to leave Canaan","A dream telling him to travel"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:1",verse:"And there was a famine in the land, beside the first famine that was in the days of Abraham. And Isaac went unto Abimelech king of the Philistines unto Gerar.",insight:"Hard seasons have a way of moving us. Isaac went to Gerar because of famine, and God met him there rather than in the place of his comfort."},
{q:"Where did the LORD tell Isaac NOT to go?",options:["Down into Egypt","Back to Ur of the Chaldees","Over to the land of Moab","Up to Damascus"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:2",verse:"And the LORD appeared unto him, and said, Go not down into Egypt; dwell in the land which I shall tell thee of:",insight:"God sometimes closes one road to keep us on His. Egypt looked like relief, but the blessing was waiting in Gerar."},
{q:"What promise did God repeat to Isaac in Gerar?",options:["I will be with thee, and will bless thee","Thou shalt be made king over Gerar","Thy flocks shall never thirst again","Thy name shall be changed to Israel"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:3",verse:"Sojourn in this land, and I will be with thee, and will bless thee; for unto thee, and unto thy seed, I will give all these countries, and I will perform the oath which I sware unto Abraham thy father;",insight:"The heart of the promise is not land or harvest but presence. I will be with thee is what makes every other blessing possible."},
{q:"To what did God compare the number of Isaac's descendants?",options:["The stars of heaven","The hairs of thy head","The stones of the field","The fish of the sea"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:4",verse:"And I will make thy seed to multiply as the stars of heaven, and will give unto thy seed all these countries; and in thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed;",insight:"God counts what we cannot. When the future looks small, He still speaks in stars."},
{q:"Why did God say He would bless Isaac?",options:["Because Abraham obeyed God's voice and kept His charge","Because Isaac built an altar before asking","Because Isaac had never sinned","Because Rebekah prayed for him daily"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:5",verse:"Because that Abraham obeyed my voice, and kept my charge, my commandments, my statutes, and my laws.",insight:"Blessing often flows downstream from obedience. Isaac inherited promises that Abraham's faithfulness had secured."},
{q:"What did Isaac claim Rebekah was, out of fear for his life?",options:["His sister","His servant","His cousin","A widow"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:7",verse:"And the men of the place asked him of his wife; and he said, She is my sister: for he feared to say, She is my wife; lest, said he, the men of the place should kill me for Rebekah; because she was fair to look upon.",insight:"Fear makes even good men lie. Isaac repeated his father's old failure, proof that courage is learned one surrender at a time."},
{q:"How did Abimelech discover the truth about Rebekah?",options:["He looked out a window and saw Isaac sporting with her","A servant confessed the secret to him","Rebekah told him herself","He heard Isaac praying aloud"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:8",verse:"And it came to pass, when he had been there a long time, that Abimelech king of the Philistines looked out at a window, and saw, and, behold, Isaac was sporting with Rebekah his wife.",insight:"Secrets rarely stay secret. What Isaac hid, a glance through a window revealed."},
{q:"What did Abimelech fear Isaac had nearly caused?",options:["Guiltiness upon the people, if one had lain with Rebekah","A famine across Gerar","A war with the Egyptians","The death of his own wife"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:10",verse:"And Abimelech said, What is this thou hast done unto us? one of the people might lightly have lien with thy wife, and thou shouldest have brought guiltiness upon us.",insight:"Our private sins can bring public guilt. Abimelech saw what Isaac missed: one man's lie could have shamed a whole people."},
{q:"What penalty did Abimelech set for touching Isaac or Rebekah?",options:["Death","Banishment from Gerar","A fine of one hundred sheep","Forty lashes"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:11",verse:"And Abimelech charged all his people, saying, He that toucheth this man or his wife shall surely be put to death.",insight:"God protected His promise even through a pagan king's decree. No one could touch the heir of the covenant."},
{q:"How much did Isaac reap in the same year he sowed?",options:["An hundredfold","A tenfold harvest","Twice what he sowed","Sevenfold"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:12",verse:"Then Isaac sowed in that land, and received in the same year an hundredfold: and the LORD blessed him.",insight:"Obedience in a hard place still yields harvest. Isaac sowed in famine soil and reaped a hundredfold, because the LORD blessed him."},
{q:"Why did the Philistines envy Isaac?",options:["He had flocks, herds, and a great store of servants","He had married the king's daughter","He had found gold in the valley","He had built the tallest tower in Gerar"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:14",verse:"For he had possession of flocks, and possession of herds, and great store of servants: and the Philistines envied him.",insight:"Blessing sometimes draws envy before it draws admiration. Isaac's prosperity made his neighbors uneasy rather than glad."},
{q:"What had the Philistines done to the wells of Abraham?",options:["Stopped them and filled them with earth","Poisoned the water in them","Given them all to Abimelech","Sold them to passing travelers"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:15",verse:"For all the wells which his father’s servants had digged in the days of Abraham his father, the Philistines had stopped them, and filled them with earth.",insight:"Envy fills in wells. The Philistines could not stop God's blessing, so they attacked the memory of it instead."},
{q:"What did Isaac name the first well, after the herdmen of Gerar strove for it?",options:["Esek","Sitnah","Rehoboth","Beersheba"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:20",verse:"And the herdmen of Gerar did strive with Isaac’s herdmen, saying, The water is ours: and he called the name of the well Esek; because they strove with him.",insight:"Sometimes faithfulness means digging again after others claim your work. Isaac named the strife honestly and kept digging."},
{q:"What did Isaac name the well over which there was no strife?",options:["Rehoboth","Esek","Sitnah","Shibah"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:22",verse:"And he removed from thence, and digged another well; and for that they strove not: and he called the name of it Rehoboth; and he said, For now the LORD hath made room for us, and we shall be fruitful in the land.",insight:"God makes room in His time. After contention and hatred, Rehoboth was Isaac's testimony that peace had finally come."},
{q:"Where did Isaac go after leaving the valley of Gerar?",options:["Up to Beersheba","Down into Egypt","Back to Haran","Over to Salem"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:23",verse:"And he went up from thence to Beer-sheba.",insight:"After the striving came the going up. Beersheba would become Isaac's place of covenant and worship."},
{q:"What did God say to Isaac at Beersheba that night?",options:["Fear not, for I am with thee","Leave this land at once","Offer thy son as a burnt offering","Number all thy flocks"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:24",verse:"And the LORD appeared unto him the same night, and said, I am the God of Abraham thy father: fear not, for I am with thee, and will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham’s sake.",insight:"God's answer to fear is always Himself. I am with thee turns a lonely night into holy ground."},
{q:"What did Isaac do first at Beersheba?",options:["Built an altar and called upon the name of the LORD","Dug three new wells","Built a walled city","Offered a tithe to Abimelech"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:25",verse:"And he builded an altar there, and called upon the name of the LORD and pitched his tent there: and there Isaac’s servants digged a well.",insight:"Isaac's first act at Beersheba was worship, not digging. The altar came before the well."},
{q:"Who came with Abimelech to make a covenant with Isaac?",options:["Ahuzzath his friend and Phichol the chief captain","Two of Isaac's own servants","The herdmen of Gerar","Esau and Jacob"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:26",verse:"Then Abimelech went to him from Gerar, and Ahuzzath one of his friends, and Phichol the chief captain of his army.",insight:"Even former enemies seek out the blessed man. Abimelech brought his friend and his general to make peace."},
{q:"What convinced Abimelech to seek a covenant with Isaac?",options:["We saw certainly that the LORD was with thee","Isaac threatened Gerar with war","A famine had struck Isaac's flocks","Abimelech had dreamed of angels"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:28",verse:"And they said, We saw certainly that the LORD was with thee: and we said, Let there be now an oath betwixt us, even betwixt us and thee, and let us make a covenant with thee;",insight:"A life lived with God becomes its own testimony. The Philistines could see what Isaac himself sometimes doubted."},
{q:"Whom did Esau marry at forty, bringing grief to Isaac and Rebekah?",options:["Judith and Bashemath, daughters of the Hittites","Mahalath and a daughter of Ishmael","Two daughters of Laban","Two Egyptian princesses"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 26:34",verse:"And Esau was forty years old when he took to wife Judith the daughter of Beeri the Hittite, and Bashemath the daughter of Elon the Hittite:",insight:"Small compromises bring lasting grief. Esau's marriages outside the covenant weighed on the whole household."}
]},
{n:27, name:"The Stolen Blessing", seal:{ref:"Genesis 27:29",verse:"Let people serve thee, and nations bow down to thee: be lord over thy brethren, and let thy mother’s sons bow down to thee: cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be he that blesseth thee."}, facts:["Did you know Jacob's name explains the whole chapter? Jacob means he grasps the heel, or deceiver, and Esau says he has lived up to it twice.","Did you know Isaac's blessing echoes God's promise to Abraham? Cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be he that blesseth thee, carries the covenant forward.","Did you know the stolen blessing could not be taken back? In the ancient world a father's spoken blessing was binding, which is why Isaac trembled when he realized what had happened."], questions:[
{q:"Why did Isaac want to bless Esau without delay?",options:["He was old and knew not the day of his death","He was leaving for Egypt","Rebekah had commanded him","Esau was departing for war"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:2",verse:"And he said, Behold now, I am old, I know not the day of my death:",insight:"Isaac felt his days shortening and hurried to bless. The awareness of mortality can sharpen what matters most."},
{q:"What did Isaac ask Esau to bring him from the field?",options:["Venison","Wild honey","Quails","A kid of the goats"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:3",verse:"Now therefore take, I pray thee, thy weapons, thy quiver and thy bow, and go out to the field, and take me some venison;",insight:"Isaac tied his blessing to a meal he loved. Appetite is a shaky foundation for spiritual decisions."},
{q:"Who overheard Isaac's plan to bless Esau?",options:["Rebekah","Jacob","A household servant","Abimelech"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:5",verse:"And Rebekah heard when Isaac spake to Esau his son. And Esau went to the field to hunt for venison, and to bring it.",insight:"One overheard conversation set this whole chapter in motion. What we do with what we hear shapes generations."},
{q:"How many kids of the goats did Rebekah tell Jacob to fetch?",options:["Two","Three","Seven","Twelve"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:9",verse:"Go now to the flock, and fetch me from thence two good kids of the goats; and I will make them savoury meat for thy father, such as he loveth:",insight:"Rebekah's plan was thorough: two choice kids, cooked to taste. Scheming often looks like careful preparation."},
{q:"What was Jacob's fear about deceiving his father?",options:["He would seem a deceiver and bring a curse upon himself","Esau would kill him on the spot","Rebekah would disown him","The meat would spoil"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:12",verse:"My father peradventure will feel me, and I shall seem to him as a deceiver; and I shall bring a curse upon me, and not a blessing.",insight:"Jacob feared being found out more than he feared deceiving. The fear of exposure is not the same as repentance."},
{q:"What did Rebekah say about the curse Jacob feared?",options:["Upon me be thy curse, my son","Curses fall only on the guilty","God will turn it into blessing","Fear not, for thy father is blind"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:13",verse:"And his mother said unto him, Upon me be thy curse, my son: only obey my voice, and go fetch me them.",insight:"Rebekah took the curse upon herself, but curses do not transfer so neatly. Her plan cost her the son she favored."},
{q:"What did Rebekah put on Jacob to make him feel like Esau?",options:["The skins of the kids of the goats on his hands and neck","Esau's armor and helmet","Wool sheared from the flock","Mud gathered from the field"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:16",verse:"And she put the skins of the kids of the goats upon his hands, and upon the smooth of his neck:",insight:"Disguise can fool hands and eyes, but it cannot fool heaven. Jacob wore Esau's skin and lost something of himself."},
{q:"What did Isaac ask to do when Jacob claimed to have found the venison so quickly?",options:["Feel him, to know whether he was his very son Esau","Smell his garments","Look upon his face","Taste the meat first"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:21",verse:"And Isaac said unto Jacob, Come near, I pray thee, that I may feel thee, my son, whether thou be my very son Esau or not.",insight:"Isaac's doubt was a mercy he ignored. When something feels wrong, it is worth pausing before pronouncing blessing."},
{q:"What did Isaac say after feeling Jacob's hands?",options:["The voice is Jacob's voice, but the hands are the hands of Esau","Thou art surely my son Esau","Bring me the meat at once","My eyes deceive me no longer"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:22",verse:"And Jacob went near unto Isaac his father; and he felt him, and said, The voice is Jacob’s voice, but the hands are the hands of Esau.",insight:"The voice told the truth the hands denied. Conscience keeps speaking even when we dress it up."},
{q:"To what did Isaac compare the smell of his son's raiment?",options:["The smell of a field which the LORD hath blessed","The cedars of Lebanon","The gardens of Egypt","Fresh rain upon the mountains"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:27",verse:"And he came near, and kissed him: and he smelled the smell of his raiment, and blessed him, and said, See, the smell of my son is as the smell of a field which the LORD hath blessed:",insight:"Isaac blessed what he smelled, not whom he knew. Decisions made by the senses alone are easily deceived."},
{q:"What three things did Isaac's blessing ask God to give?",options:["Dew of heaven, fatness of the earth, and plenty of corn and wine","Gold, silver, and precious stones","Many wives and a long life","Victory in every battle"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:28",verse:"Therefore God give thee of the dew of heaven, and the fatness of the earth, and plenty of corn and wine:",insight:"The blessing asked heaven and earth to serve Jacob. True abundance is dew from above before it is harvest below."},
{q:"According to the blessing, who would bow down to Jacob?",options:["Nations and his mother's sons","Only the Philistines","The angels of heaven","No one at all"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:29",verse:"Let people serve thee, and nations bow down to thee: be lord over thy brethren, and let thy mother’s sons bow down to thee: cursed be every one that curseth thee, and blessed be he that blesseth thee.",insight:"God's choice, not birth order, set Jacob above. The blessing confirmed what God had told Rebekah years before."},
{q:"What did Isaac say when he learned the blessing was already given?",options:["Yea, and he shall be blessed","I take it back at once","Then let Esau share it","The blessing is void"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:33",verse:"And Isaac trembled very exceedingly, and said, Who? where is he that hath taken venison, and brought it me, and I have eaten of all before thou camest, and have blessed him? yea, and he shall be blessed.",insight:"Even trembling, Isaac would not undo what God had ordained. The blessing stood because God stood behind it."},
{q:"How did Esau cry out when he heard the blessing was gone?",options:["With a great and exceeding bitter cry","With laughter at the trick","In silence with a bowed head","With a song of praise"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:34",verse:"And when Esau heard the words of his father, he cried with a great and exceeding bitter cry, and said unto his father, Bless me, even me also, O my father.",insight:"Esau's tears were real, but they came too late. He had despised his birthright long before he wept for the blessing."},
{q:"What did Esau say Jacob had taken from him two times?",options:["His birthright and his blessing","His wives and his flocks","His bow and his quiver","His tent and his servants"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:36",verse:"And he said, Is not he rightly named Jacob? for he hath supplanted me these two times: he took away my birthright; and, behold, now he hath taken away my blessing. And he said, Hast thou not reserved a blessing for me?",insight:"Esau finally saw the pattern: birthright, then blessing. What we trade cheaply, we later mourn dearly."},
{q:"What did Esau beg when Isaac said the blessing was spent?",options:["Hast thou but one blessing, my father? bless me, even me also","Give me Jacob's portion instead","Curse Jacob for me","Let me die childless"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:38",verse:"And Esau said unto his father, Hast thou but one blessing, my father? bless me, even me also, O my father. And Esau lifted up his voice, and wept.",insight:"Esau begged for a blessing he had not valued. Desire after loss is not the same as devotion before it."},
{q:"By what did Isaac say Esau would live?",options:["By his sword","By his flocks","By the dew of heaven","By his wisdom"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:40",verse:"And by thy sword shalt thou live, and shalt serve thy brother; and it shall come to pass when thou shalt have the dominion, that thou shalt break his yoke from off thy neck.",insight:"Esau's portion was the sword and a restless dominion. A life lived by force finds little rest."},
{q:"What did Esau plan to do after the days of mourning for his father?",options:["Slay his brother Jacob","Flee to Egypt with traders","Steal the birthright back","Burn Jacob's tents"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:41",verse:"And Esau hated Jacob because of the blessing wherewith his father blessed him: and Esau said in his heart, The days of mourning for my father are at hand; then will I slay my brother Jacob.",insight:"Bitterness plans murder while waiting for a funeral. Esau's hatred grew in the shadow of his father's coming death."},
{q:"Where did Rebekah tell Jacob to flee?",options:["To Laban her brother in Haran","To Egypt with the traders","To Beersheba alone","To the mountains of Seir"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:43",verse:"Now therefore, my son, obey my voice; and arise, flee thou to Laban my brother to Haran;",insight:"Jacob's deception bought him a blessing and cost him home. He fled with the promise but without his family."},
{q:"Why did Rebekah say she was weary of her life?",options:["Because of the daughters of Heth","Because of Isaac's blindness","Because of the famine","Because Jacob had fled"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 27:46",verse:"And Rebekah said to Isaac, I am weary of my life because of the daughters of Heth: if Jacob take a wife of the daughters of Heth, such as these which are of the daughters of the land, what good shall my life do me?",insight:"Rebekah's weariness became the cover for Jacob's escape. God used even her complaint to move His plan forward."}
]},
{n:28, name:"Jacob's Ladder", seal:{ref:"Genesis 28:15",verse:"And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest, and will bring thee again into this land; for I will not leave thee, until I have done that which I have spoken to thee of."}, facts:["Did you know Bethel means house of God? Jacob named it after waking from his dream of the ladder, and it became one of Israel's most important holy places.","Did you know Jacob's vow is the first promise of a tithe in the Bible? Of all that thou shalt give me I will surely give the tenth unto thee.","Did you know the ladder's angels were ascending first, then descending? Heaven was already at work around Jacob before he ever climbed."], questions:[
{q:"What did Isaac charge Jacob NOT to do?",options:["Take a wife of the daughters of Canaan","Return to Beersheba","Take any of Esau's flocks","Build an altar in Haran"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:1",verse:"And Isaac called Jacob, and blessed him, and charged him, and said unto him, Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan.",insight:"Isaac's charge guarded the covenant line. Who we marry shapes the faith of generations."},
{q:"Where did Isaac send Jacob to find a wife?",options:["To Padanaram, to the house of Bethuel","To Egypt, to Pharaoh's court","To Gerar, to Abimelech","To Salem, to Melchizedek"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:2",verse:"Arise, go to Padan-aram, to the house of Bethuel thy mother’s father; and take thee a wife from thence of the daughters of Laban thy mother’s brother.",insight:"Jacob was sent back to the family's roots to find a wife. Sometimes the way forward runs through the past."},
{q:"What name of God did Isaac use when blessing Jacob?",options:["God Almighty","The LORD of hosts","The Everlasting God","The Most High God"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:3",verse:"And God Almighty bless thee, and make thee fruitful, and multiply thee, that thou mayest be a multitude of people;",insight:"El Shaddai, the All-Sufficient One, was the name Isaac invoked. Jacob would need that sufficiency on the road ahead."},
{q:"What was Jacob to inherit, according to Isaac's blessing?",options:["The land of his pilgrimage, which God gave to Abraham","The wells of Beersheba","Esau's portion of the flocks","The city of Luz"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:4",verse:"And give thee the blessing of Abraham, to thee, and to thy seed with thee; that thou mayest inherit the land wherein thou art a stranger, which God gave unto Abraham.",insight:"The blessing of Abraham was never just about land. It was about belonging to God's promise in a foreign world."},
{q:"Who was Laban to Jacob?",options:["His mother's brother","His father's cousin","Esau's father in law","His grandfather's servant"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:5",verse:"And Isaac sent away Jacob: and he went to Padan-aram unto Laban, son of Bethuel the Syrian, the brother of Rebekah, Jacob’s and Esau’s mother.",insight:"Jacob left with nothing but a blessing and a destination. God often sends us out lighter than we would choose."},
{q:"Whose daughter did Esau take as wife when he saw the daughters of Canaan displeased Isaac?",options:["Mahalath, the daughter of Ishmael","Judith, the daughter of Beeri","Adah, the daughter of Elon","Bashemath, the daughter of Elon"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:9",verse:"Then went Esau unto Ishmael, and took unto the wives which he had Mahalath the daughter of Ishmael Abraham’s son, the sister of Nebajoth, to be his wife.",insight:"Esau tried to please his father by adding a wife, but more of the wrong thing never fixes the heart."},
{q:"From where did Jacob set out, and toward where did he go?",options:["From Beersheba toward Haran","From Gerar toward Egypt","From Hebron toward Salem","From Bethel toward Padanaram"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:10",verse:"And Jacob went out from Beer-sheba, and went toward Haran.",insight:"Jacob walked away from everything familiar. The road to Haran became the road to God."},
{q:"What did Jacob use for his pillows that night?",options:["Stones of that place","His bundled cloak","A saddle from his camel","Sheaves of wheat"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:11",verse:"And he lighted upon a certain place, and tarried there all night, because the sun was set; and he took of the stones of that place, and put them for his pillows, and lay down in that place to sleep.",insight:"A fugitive's pillow became a sanctuary. God meets us in the most uncomfortable places."},
{q:"What did Jacob see in his dream?",options:["A ladder set up on the earth, its top reaching to heaven","A burning bush that was not consumed","A chariot of fire in the clouds","Seven fat and seven lean kine"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:12",verse:"And he dreamed, and behold a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven: and behold the angels of God ascending and descending on it.",insight:"Heaven was not far after all. The ladder showed Jacob that God comes down before He asks us to climb."},
{q:"What were the angels of God doing on the ladder?",options:["Ascending and descending on it","Singing around its base","Guarding its top with swords","Carrying stones to build it"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:12",verse:"And he dreamed, and behold a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven: and behold the angels of God ascending and descending on it.",insight:"The angels were already ascending before they descended. God's care for Jacob was active before he ever woke."},
{q:"How did the LORD introduce Himself above the ladder?",options:["I am the LORD God of Abraham thy father, and the God of Isaac","I am the God of Bethel","I am thy shield and exceeding great reward","I am that I am"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:13",verse:"And, behold, the LORD stood above it, and said, I am the LORD God of Abraham thy father, and the God of Isaac: the land whereon thou liest, to thee will I give it, and to thy seed;",insight:"God introduced Himself as the God of Jacob's fathers. The fugitive was still the heir."},
{q:"To what did God compare the number of Jacob's seed?",options:["The dust of the earth","The stars of heaven","The sand of the sea","The grass of the field"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:14",verse:"And thy seed shall be as the dust of the earth, and thou shalt spread abroad to the west, and to the east, and to the north, and to the south: and in thee and in thy seed shall all the families of the earth be blessed.",insight:"From a lonely man with a stone pillow, God promised descendants like dust. God's arithmetic starts with surrender."},
{q:"In whom did God say all the families of the earth would be blessed?",options:["In Jacob and in his seed","In Esau and his sons","In Laban's household","In the angels on the ladder"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:14",verse:"And thy seed shall be as the dust of the earth, and thou shalt spread abroad to the west, and to the east, and to the north, and to the south: and in thee and in thy seed shall all the families of the earth be blessed.",insight:"The promise stretched beyond Jacob to all families of the earth. One man's dream carried the world's hope."},
{q:"What did God promise Jacob about His presence?",options:["I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest","I will send thee back within seven days","I will appear to Laban in thy stead","I will blind the eyes of Esau"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:15",verse:"And, behold, I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest, and will bring thee again into this land; for I will not leave thee, until I have done that which I have spoken to thee of.",insight:"This is the traveler's psalm: I am with thee, I will keep thee, I will bring thee again. God promised the whole journey."},
{q:"What did Jacob say when he awoke from his sleep?",options:["Surely the LORD is in this place; and I knew it not","I must return to my father at once","The angels have forsaken me","This was but an empty dream"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:16",verse:"And Jacob awaked out of his sleep, and he said, Surely the LORD is in this place; and I knew it not.",insight:"God is often present where we least expect Him. Jacob's surprise is an invitation to look again at our own nights."},
{q:"What did Jacob call the place where he had dreamed?",options:["The house of God, and the gate of heaven","The mountain of the LORD","The field of angels","The city of peace"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:17",verse:"And he was afraid, and said, How dreadful is this place! this is none other but the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven.",insight:"Awe is the right response to grace. Jacob called the place dreadful and beautiful in the same breath."},
{q:"What did Jacob do with the stone in the morning?",options:["Set it up for a pillar and poured oil upon it","Broke it into twelve pieces","Buried it where he had slept","Carried it with him to Haran"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:18",verse:"And Jacob rose up early in the morning, and took the stone that he had put for his pillows, and set it up for a pillar, and poured oil upon the top of it.",insight:"Jacob marked the spot so he would not forget. Memorials help memory hold what the heart felt."},
{q:"What new name did Jacob give to the place?",options:["Bethel","Beersheba","Peniel","Mizpah"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:19",verse:"And he called the name of that place Bethel: but the name of that city was called Luz at the first.",insight:"Luz became Bethel, the house of God. An ordinary place becomes holy when God shows up."},
{q:"What did Jacob vow to give God of all he received?",options:["The tenth","The firstborn of his flocks","Seven lambs","Half of his wages"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:22",verse:"And this stone, which I have set for a pillar, shall be God’s house: and of all that thou shalt give me I will surely give the tenth unto thee.",insight:"Jacob's first recorded vow was about giving. Gratitude that has met God starts planning generosity."},
{q:"What did Jacob say the stone would become?",options:["God's house","An altar to Isaac","A memorial to Rachel","A boundary marker"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 28:22",verse:"And this stone, which I have set for a pillar, shall be God’s house: and of all that thou shalt give me I will surely give the tenth unto thee.",insight:"The stone pillow became God's house. What we surrender to Him, He consecrates."}
]},
{n:29, name:"Jacob's Wives", seal:{ref:"Genesis 29:35",verse:"And she conceived again, and bare a son: and she said, Now will I praise the LORD: therefore she called his name Judah; and left bearing."}, facts:["Did you know Jacob met Rachel at a well, just as Abraham's servant met Rebekah at a well? Wells were the meeting place for brides in Genesis.","Did you know Leah, the unloved wife, became the mother of Judah? Through her son came King David and, generations later, Jesus.","Did you know Jacob worked fourteen years for Rachel? Seven before the wedding he thought was hers, and seven after Laban's deception."], questions:[
{q:"How many flocks of sheep were lying by the well when Jacob arrived?",options:["Three","Two","Seven","Twelve"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:2",verse:"And he looked, and behold a well in the field, and, lo, there were three flocks of sheep lying by it; for out of that well they watered the flocks: and a great stone was upon the well’s mouth.",insight:"Providence had arranged the scene before Jacob arrived. Three flocks, one well, and Rachel on the way."},
{q:"What covered the mouth of the well?",options:["A great stone","A wooden lid","A bronze gate","A thorn bush"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:2",verse:"And he looked, and behold a well in the field, and, lo, there were three flocks of sheep lying by it; for out of that well they watered the flocks: and a great stone was upon the well’s mouth.",insight:"The great stone kept the well for all until all were gathered. Some blessings are held in common until the right time."},
{q:"From where did the shepherds say they were?",options:["Haran","Gerar","Beersheba","Damascus"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:4",verse:"And Jacob said unto them, My brethren, whence be ye? And they said, Of Haran are we.",insight:"Jacob had walked some five hundred miles to find his mother's people. The first voices he heard were from home."},
{q:"Who was coming with the sheep as Jacob spoke with the shepherds?",options:["Rachel, Laban's daughter","Leah with her handmaid","Zilpah with water","Bilhah with bread"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:6",verse:"And he said unto them, Is he well? And they said, He is well: and, behold, Rachel his daughter cometh with the sheep.",insight:"God timed the conversation perfectly. As Jacob asked about Laban, Rachel appeared with the sheep."},
{q:"Why could the shepherds not water the sheep yet?",options:["Until all the flocks were gathered and the stone rolled away","Until Laban himself arrived","Until the sun had fully set","Until Jacob paid them"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:8",verse:"And they said, We cannot, until all the flocks be gathered together, and till they roll the stone from the well’s mouth; then we water the sheep.",insight:"The shepherds waited for all before watering any. Community has its rhythms, and Jacob learned to honor them."},
{q:"What did Jacob do when he saw Rachel with Laban's flock?",options:["Rolled the stone from the well's mouth and watered the flock","Built an altar beside the well","Ran at once to embrace Laban","Offered a sacrifice of thanksgiving"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:10",verse:"And it came to pass, when Jacob saw Rachel the daughter of Laban his mother’s brother, and the sheep of Laban his mother’s brother, that Jacob went near, and rolled the stone from the well’s mouth, and watered the flock of Laban his mother’s brother.",insight:"Jacob's strength served love's errand. He moved for Rachel what many men together usually moved."},
{q:"How did Jacob greet Rachel?",options:["He kissed her, lifted up his voice, and wept","He bowed himself seven times","He gave her a ring of gold","He washed her feet"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:11",verse:"And Jacob kissed Rachel, and lifted up his voice, and wept.",insight:"Joy and tears arrived together. After the lonely road, family felt like water in the desert."},
{q:"How did Laban greet Jacob when he heard the tidings?",options:["He ran to meet him, embraced him, and kissed him","He sent servants out with gifts","He made him wait seven days","He tested him with riddles"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:13",verse:"And it came to pass, when Laban heard the tidings of Jacob his sister’s son, that he ran to meet him, and embraced him, and kissed him, and brought him to his house. And he told Laban all these things.",insight:"Laban ran to meet the son of his sister. Hospitality in that world was wholehearted and physical."},
{q:"What did Laban say after Jacob had stayed a month?",options:["Surely thou art my bone and my flesh","Thou art my servant forever","Go back to thy father","Pay me for thy lodging"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:14",verse:"And Laban said to him, Surely thou art my bone and my flesh. And he abode with him the space of a month.",insight:"Laban named the kinship before naming the wages. Family first, business second, was the order of the day."},
{q:"What wages did Jacob ask for his service?",options:["Seven years of service for Rachel","One hundred sheep for Leah","Silver for a bride price","A well of his own"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:18",verse:"And Jacob loved Rachel; and said, I will serve thee seven years for Rachel thy younger daughter.",insight:"Love set the price and counted it joy. Seven years of labor was Jacob's dowry for Rachel."},
{q:"How did the seven years of service seem to Jacob?",options:["But a few days, for the love he had to her","Longer than his whole life","Like a heavy burden","As nothing at all, for he forgot them"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:20",verse:"And Jacob served seven years for Rachel; and they seemed unto him but a few days, for the love he had to her.",insight:"Love transforms time. Seven years felt like days because every day worked toward Rachel."},
{q:"Whom did Laban bring to Jacob on the wedding night?",options:["Leah","Rachel","Zilpah","Bilhah"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:23",verse:"And it came to pass in the evening, that he took Leah his daughter, and brought her to him; and he went in unto her.",insight:"Deception came back to the deceiver. Jacob, who had worn his brother's skin, was given the wrong bride in the dark."},
{q:"What excuse did Laban give for giving Leah first?",options:["It must not be so done in our country, to give the younger before the firstborn","Rachel was too young to marry","Leah had begged him in tears","The men of the place demanded it"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:26",verse:"And Laban said, It must not be so done in our country, to give the younger before the firstborn.",insight:"Laban used Jacob's own birth order against him. The trickster met a better trickster."},
{q:"How many more years did Jacob agree to serve for Rachel?",options:["Seven","Three","Fourteen","Ten"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:27",verse:"Fulfil her week, and we will give thee this also for the service which thou shalt serve with me yet seven other years.",insight:"Jacob's love did not calculate the extra cost. He served another seven years without bargaining."},
{q:"Which wife did Jacob love more?",options:["Rachel","Leah","He loved them equally","Neither, he loved only his flocks"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:30",verse:"And he went in also unto Rachel, and he loved also Rachel more than Leah, and served with him yet seven other years.",insight:"A divided heart made a divided home. Loving one wife more sowed years of sorrow."},
{q:"Why did the LORD open Leah's womb?",options:["When He saw that Leah was hated","Because Jacob prayed for her","Because Laban asked it of Him","Because Rachel was too young"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:31",verse:"And when the LORD saw that Leah was hated, he opened her womb: but Rachel was barren.",insight:"God saw the unloved wife. When no one else chose Leah, the LORD opened her womb."},
{q:"Why did Leah name her first son Reuben?",options:["Surely the LORD hath looked upon my affliction","Because the LORD hath heard that I was hated","Now will my husband be joined unto me","Now will I praise the LORD"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:32",verse:"And Leah conceived, and bare a son, and she called his name Reuben: for she said, Surely the LORD hath looked upon my affliction; now therefore my husband will love me.",insight:"Leah named her hope into her son: surely now my husband will love me. Affliction taught her to look to the LORD."},
{q:"Why was the second son named Simeon?",options:["Because the LORD hath heard that I was hated","Surely the LORD hath looked upon my affliction","Now will my husband dwell with me","God hath judged me"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:33",verse:"And she conceived again, and bare a son; and said, Because the LORD hath heard that I was hated, he hath therefore given me this son also: and she called his name Simeon.",insight:"God hears the hated. Simeon's name was Leah's testimony that heaven had listened."},
{q:"Why was the third son named Levi?",options:["Now this time will my husband be joined unto me","The LORD shall add to me another son","A troop cometh","Happy am I"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:34",verse:"And she conceived again, and bare a son; and said, Now this time will my husband be joined unto me, because I have born him three sons: therefore was his name called Levi.",insight:"Three sons in, Leah still longed to be joined to her husband. Even blessings can leave the heart's ache unmet."},
{q:"Why did Leah name her fourth son Judah?",options:["Now will I praise the LORD","God hath given me my hire","With great wrestlings have I wrestled","Surely the LORD hath looked upon my affliction"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 29:35",verse:"And she conceived again, and bare a son: and she said, Now will I praise the LORD: therefore she called his name Judah; and left bearing.",insight:"Leah stopped striving and started praising. From Now will I praise the LORD came Judah, forefather of the Messiah."}
]},
{n:30, name:"Sons and Flocks", seal:{ref:"Genesis 30:24",verse:"And she called his name Joseph; and said, The LORD shall add to me another son."}, facts:["Did you know eleven of Jacob's twelve sons were born in this stretch of the story? Only Benjamin comes later, on the road to Bethlehem.","Did you know the name Joseph means may he add? Rachel's prayer was answered again when Benjamin was born.","Did you know mandrakes were believed to help with childbearing in the ancient world? That is why Rachel bargained a night with Jacob for Leah's mandrakes."], questions:[
{q:"What did Rachel say to Jacob when she saw she bore him no children?",options:["Give me children, or else I die","Send me back to my father","Take another wife for thyself","Let Leah pray for me"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:1",verse:"And when Rachel saw that she bare Jacob no children, Rachel envied her sister; and said unto Jacob, Give me children, or else I die.",insight:"Rachel's desperation was real but misplaced. Children are a gift, not a possession to demand."},
{q:"How did Jacob answer Rachel's demand?",options:["Am I in God's stead, who hath withheld from thee the fruit of the womb?","Be patient yet seven more years","Ask Laban for a blessing","Go and wash in the river"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:2",verse:"And Jacob’s anger was kindled against Rachel: and he said, Am I in God’s stead, who hath withheld from thee the fruit of the womb?",insight:"Jacob's anger spoke truth roughly: only God opens the womb. Even sharp words can carry right theology."},
{q:"Whom did Rachel give to Jacob to wife?",options:["Bilhah her handmaid","Zilpah her handmaid","Dinah her sister","A daughter of Heth"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:4",verse:"And she gave him Bilhah her handmaid to wife: and Jacob went in unto her.",insight:"Rachel borrowed the world's solution to a spiritual ache. Custom is not covenant, even when the custom is old."},
{q:"Why did Rachel name Bilhah's first son Dan?",options:["God hath judged me, and hath heard my voice","With great wrestlings have I wrestled","God hath taken away my reproach","A troop cometh"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:6",verse:"And Rachel said, God hath judged me, and hath also heard my voice, and hath given me a son: therefore called she his name Dan.",insight:"Rachel heard vindication in Dan's birth: God hath judged me. We often read our own verdicts into God's gifts."},
{q:"Why was Bilhah's second son named Naphtali?",options:["With great wrestlings have I wrestled with my sister, and I have prevailed","God hath judged me","Happy am I, for the daughters will call me blessed","God hath given me my hire"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:8",verse:"And Rachel said, With great wrestlings have I wrestled with my sister, and I have prevailed: and she called his name Naphtali.",insight:"Rachel named her wrestling match with her sister. Rivalry, not rest, marked the nursery."},
{q:"Whom did Leah give to Jacob when she had left bearing?",options:["Zilpah her maid","Bilhah her maid","Her daughter Dinah","A Canaanite servant"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:9",verse:"When Leah saw that she had left bearing, she took Zilpah her maid, and gave her Jacob to wife.",insight:"Leah answered rivalry with rivalry. The competition of the wives multiplied children but not peace."},
{q:"Why did Leah name Zilpah's first son Gad?",options:["A troop cometh","Happy am I","God hath judged me","The LORD shall add"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:11",verse:"And Leah said, A troop cometh: and she called his name Gad.",insight:"A troop cometh: Leah greeted another son as fortune. Even in rivalry, she saw God's hand."},
{q:"Why was Zilpah's second son named Asher?",options:["Happy am I, for the daughters will call me blessed","A troop cometh","God hath given me my hire","Now will my husband dwell with me"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:13",verse:"And Leah said, Happy am I, for the daughters will call me blessed: and she called his name Asher.",insight:"Happy am I, said Leah, for the daughters will call me blessed. The approval of others is a thin foundation for joy."},
{q:"What did Reuben find in the field in the days of wheat harvest?",options:["Mandrakes","Wild grapes","A lost lamb","A ring of gold"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:14",verse:"And Reuben went in the days of wheat harvest, and found mandrakes in the field, and brought them unto his mother Leah. Then Rachel said to Leah, Give me, I pray thee, of thy son’s mandrakes.",insight:"Reuben's mandrakes became currency between sisters. What was meant as love's fruit became a bargaining chip."},
{q:"What did Rachel offer Leah in exchange for the mandrakes?",options:["That Jacob would lie with Leah that night","Seven more years of service","Half of her flocks","Her handmaid Bilhah"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:15",verse:"And she said unto her, Is it a small matter that thou hast taken my husband? and wouldest thou take away my son’s mandrakes also? And Rachel said, Therefore he shall lie with thee to night for thy son’s mandrakes.",insight:"Rachel traded a night for mandrakes. When children become commodities, everyone loses."},
{q:"Why did Leah name her fifth son Issachar?",options:["God hath given me my hire, because I have given my maiden to my husband","Now will my husband dwell with me","God hath taken away my reproach","With great wrestlings have I wrestled"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:18",verse:"And Leah said, God hath given me my hire, because I have given my maiden to my husband: and she called his name Issachar.",insight:"God hath given me my hire, said Leah. She saw her son as wages for a hard bargain, yet God gave graciously."},
{q:"Why was the sixth son named Zebulun?",options:["Now will my husband dwell with me, because I have born him six sons","God hath given me my hire","Happy am I","A troop cometh"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:20",verse:"And Leah said, God hath endued me with a good dowry; now will my husband dwell with me, because I have born him six sons: and she called his name Zebulun.",insight:"Six sons, and Leah still hoped her husband would dwell with her. Achievements rarely win the love we crave."},
{q:"What was the name of Leah's daughter?",options:["Dinah","Mahalath","Bashemath","Judith"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:21",verse:"And afterwards she bare a daughter, and called her name Dinah.",insight:"Dinah is named with no explanation, only love. Not every child in the story needs a rivalry to be remembered."},
{q:"What did Rachel say when Joseph was born?",options:["God hath taken away my reproach","Now will I praise the LORD","God hath judged me","Happy am I"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:23",verse:"And she conceived, and bare a son; and said, God hath taken away my reproach:",insight:"Barrenness felt like shame in Rachel's world. Joseph's birth lifted a reproach she had carried for years."},
{q:"What did the name Joseph mean in Rachel's hope?",options:["The LORD shall add to me another son","God hath taken away my reproach","Now will my husband dwell with me","A troop cometh"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:24",verse:"And she called his name Joseph; and said, The LORD shall add to me another son.",insight:"Rachel named her hope forward: The LORD shall add to me another son. Gratitude that asks for more is still faith."},
{q:"What did Laban say he had learned by experience?",options:["That the LORD had blessed him for Jacob's sake","That Jacob was deceiving him","That the flocks were dying","That Rachel was barren"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:27",verse:"And Laban said unto him, I pray thee, if I have found favour in thine eyes, tarry: for I have learned by experience that the LORD hath blessed me for thy sake.",insight:"Even Laban could see it: the LORD had blessed him for Jacob's sake. God's favor on His people spills over."},
{q:"Which animals did Jacob ask for as his wages?",options:["The speckled and spotted cattle, and the brown among the sheep","Only the white lambs","The camels and the asses","Every firstborn of the flock"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:32",verse:"I will pass through all thy flock to day, removing from thence all the speckled and spotted cattle, and all the brown cattle among the sheep, and the spotted and speckled among the goats: and of such shall be my hire.",insight:"Jacob asked only for the marked animals, the minority. He trusted God to prosper him without taking what was Laban's."},
{q:"What did Jacob place before the flocks at the watering troughs?",options:["Rods of poplar, hazel, and chestnut with white strakes pilled in them","Stones carried from Bethel","Branches of olive","His own staff"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:37",verse:"And Jacob took him rods of green poplar, and of the hazel and chesnut tree; and pilled white strakes in them, and made the white appear which was in the rods.",insight:"The pilled rods were Jacob's strange strategy. Whether craft or faith, God made the flocks conceive as He willed."},
{q:"What kind of cattle did the flocks bring forth before the rods?",options:["Ringstraked, speckled, and spotted","Only pure white","Only solid black","Twins every single time"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:39",verse:"And the flocks conceived before the rods, and brought forth cattle ringstraked, speckled, and spotted.",insight:"The flocks bore marked young, and Jacob's wages multiplied. God honors honest work, even under a hard master."},
{q:"How did Jacob's fortunes stand at the end of this chapter?",options:["The man increased exceedingly, with much cattle and servants","He lost all his wages to Laban","He fled by night with nothing","He returned to his father poor"],a:0,ref:"Genesis 30:43",verse:"And the man increased exceedingly, and had much cattle, and maidservants, and menservants, and camels, and asses.",insight:"The man increased exceedingly. Twenty years of labor ended with flocks, servants, and camels, all by God's hand."}
]}
]);
